#{[#edb/ref 17592186050271 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093116} :activity/id #uuid "9ed557a5-e06f-5f5a-9230-32a93a80b727" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093369 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can be used to differentiate exponential functions like \n\n$$\ny = e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u = 3x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=3x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092116 :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-727" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{e}^{5x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092118 :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/math-academy-id "e-728" :question/problem "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}ln⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}ln⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}ln⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092119 :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-729" :question/problem "Given that $y=e^{-x^2},$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093118}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092399}] [#edb/ref 17592186050283 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120618} :activity/id #uuid "3366a432-0200-5657-84c5-2f4c70949b50" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121017 :tutorial/content "Recall that a matrix is square if the number of rows equals the number of columns. There are other types of square matrices with special names.\n\n- An **upper triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *below* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all upper triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and above.\n\n- A **lower triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *above* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all lower triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAgain, note that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and below." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119585 :question/id #uuid "fe3edf12-af5b-5bb0-b5b3-efb6cd809134" :question/math-academy-id "e-4394" :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n![](images/fc/fc2ed708d717844ca73b7ecbfd8467923fad091ae524153350e7e2fb3df1ea4e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above the diagonal are $0$'s:\n![](images/52/5260137f2dbf20b44400659be8467da333ff228e5bff323465f920a9505ee5f2.png)\n\nTherefore, it is a lower triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not an upper triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element below the diagonal: $a_{21}=6 \\neq 0.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Triangular Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119586 :question/id #uuid "d7c263d4-5d0a-52cd-b876-cfa3bd3171e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4395" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are lower triangular matrices?\n\n![](images/ad/ad9636de9b8c93338350a6b99d88476b481ed1bf03a5dd64935abedbdb626524.png)" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is a lower triangular matrix since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nThe matrices $A$ and $C$ are *not* lower triangular matrices since they each have a non-zero element above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23} & =1\\ne 0 \\\\ {c}_{13} & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Triangular Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119598 :question/id #uuid "205d32a2-ac26-5eaf-896a-c6182a70eff7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4498" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n![](images/4d/4dd9b1c1b3d789c166ecd8f6d2a909609c7f4324705c84115cce84a29181bf32.png)\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is upper triangular\n\n- $A$ is lower triangular\n\n- $A^T-A$ is lower triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $A$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n![](images/c1/c1ec838cd30cca8fbb2e3996d5a7d84dadb233e1951d05b532a9d606d887eb2f.png)\n\n- Statement II is true, since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros:\n![](images/f3/f39c68a9b1f5e16caffbf159b629a958e147e14099334b049bdfcd857eef84d2.png)\n\n- Statement III is false, since the result of $A^T-A$ has non-zero entries above the main diagonal:\n![](images/f4/f4415c87201f5b92f23e3320c313cd58ac4e720135928645896f976614c5eaa0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Statements About a Matrix"}}] :activity/title "Triangular Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119999}] [#edb/ref 17592186050289 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134750} :activity/id #uuid "c1d7ccaa-f5c6-5920-a397-e8a258f25c51" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133642 :question/id #uuid "132af6fa-454c-596c-a057-dfe531792102" :question/math-academy-id "e-6283" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz}$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,\\sqrt 2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t) =t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k},$ then along the curve we have $x = t^2,$ $y = t,$ and $z=1.$  \n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t\\cdot 1}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u= 4t^2+1,$ $\\textrm d u=8t\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[{9}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[27-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 26 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Using a Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135075 :tutorial/content "We wish to derive the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we proceed in four steps, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We break the integration path $C$ into a series of line segments.\n\nWe choose a finite number of points $P_0,$ $P_1,$ $\\ldots,$ $P_n$ along $C$ and form the polygonal path\n\n$$\n\\overline{P_0P_1} \\cup \\overline{P_1P_2} \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n}.\n$$\n\nOur polygonal path is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d320e6a0f63a823e049203203cbc13c210e80bc98aab5a53bcb4e5299240920.png)\n\n**Step 2**: For each line segment, we approximate the area enclosed by $f(x,y)$ and $C$ between the two endpoints of the segment. To do this, we calculate the area of a rectangle formed by the segment and the surface $z=f(x,y).$\n\nThe width of each line segment is given by\n\n$$\n||\\overline{P_{k-1}P_k}|| .\n$$\n\nIf $P_k$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t_k),$ then by the mean value theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{k-1}{P}_{k}}‖ & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖({t}_{k}-{t}_{k-1}) \\\\  & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖Δ{t}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some intermediate point $c_k \\in [t_{k-1}, t_k],$ where $\\Delta t_k = t_k - t_{k-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the area formed by the rectangle with a width of $\\big\\| \\overline{P_{k-1}P_k} \\big\\|$ and a height of $f(c_k)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(c_k) \\| \\mathbf r'(c_k)\\|\\Delta t_k.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We sum the areas of all of our small rectangles, which gives us an *approximation* of our line integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s \\approx \\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\mathbf r(c_k)) \\| \\mathbf{r}'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We take the limit as the width of each rectangle tends to zero (or equivalently, as the number of rectangles tends to infinity). This gives us the exact representation of our line integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty }\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\mathbf r(c_k)) \\| \\mathbf{r}'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k = \\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$" :tutorial/title "Derivation of the  Line Integral Formula"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133644 :question/id #uuid "f07dd3d3-606f-520f-a95e-04303aa32f24" :question/math-academy-id "e-6307" :question/problem "Find an integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)= \\left \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f53d78debfec5f8c50e0f3a5947d2a36126e18c073d7b0cc4c59d506753059b3.png)\n\nThe path of integration is shown above. We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $f(x,y) =  xy^3$. Along the path, we have $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t,$ and so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3} \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int^{\\pi/2}_{0} \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, \\textrm{d}t .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function as a Definite Integral"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133645 :question/id #uuid "8bf6a863-9b9f-5877-84bc-3e11d71261c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6308" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C}xy\\,\\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y=4t.$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =xy,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3t\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{2}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =20{[{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135080 :tutorial/content "A **line integral** $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s$ is similar to a definite integral, but for a function of multiple variables along a general path $C.$\n\nTo understand the intuition behind line integrals, remember that the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ can be interpreted as the (signed) area bounded between the function $y=f(x)$ and the $x$-axis between $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/93/93ce31caadb9d753a13acc2f25b898738aa67b70f1374816fccfc0cf3be21b7a.png)\n\nIntuitively, we can think of the integral as the result of summing the areas of many small rectangles that we accumulate as we move along the $x$-axis on the **path** from $x=a$ to $x=b.$\n\nWe can also draw paths as general curves in the plane. For example, consider the path $C,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, 2\\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t\\in \\left[ 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nThis path represents a quarter circle of radius $2$ traversed counterclockwise from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2)$ in the first quadrant:\n\n![](images/6d/6d933c280ebb530aefaadc1b5ef927e39603635960348255045c138ee9bab366.png)\n\nLet $f(x,y)= 3 - \\sin (2xy) .$ If we sum the areas of the thin rectangles that are formed by the curve $C$ and the surface $z=f(x,y)$ as we move along the curve $C$ from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2),$ we get a *signed area,* as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4c/4c3b973883d1943eebc47d168f6c2fcf00cf50702ce9afdfec7d5031735bbbff.png)\n\nThis signed area is equal to the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.$\n\nFinally, we sometimes call $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s$ the line integral of $f$ **with respect to arc length.** Other types of line integral are possible, as we'll see soon." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135081 :tutorial/content "Let $C$ be a \"smooth\" curve, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{r}(t), \\qquad t \\in (a,b).\n$$\n\nNow let $f(x,y)$ be a continuous function that is defined everywhere on $C.$ The **line integral** of $f$ over $C,$ denoted \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\ncan be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe line integral formula extends to functions of $n$ variables. For example, for functions of three variables, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll go through the details of where this formula comes from at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at computing line integrals." :tutorial/title "The General Formula"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134744}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133993}] [#edb/ref 17592186050332 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176807} :activity/id #uuid "eb431efc-453b-5866-adc7-559d3c697e3e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838 :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle.$ \nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate ![](images/f1/f16fa431ecef10f5538168a49f4f3ceb4ec827114cab6854142fc9ca4d9ce9af.svg) where the path ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n![](images/ac/accf185e064f4c9c99cc6ad1437aabc7d5471cc332ff6c38d51a3f11ec6e26f2.svg)\n\nIn our case, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) and its boundary ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n![](images/8c/8cbe1833f3c6f1612d69aecc8354e66cbf24ef426d648ed5c90224bbcf5ab341.svg)\nwe obtain\n\n![](images/01/01e3a66875647bfc9704bc6a1066dbc18c5cf1c0b0144aff8eb2e478685f83d2.svg)\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/49/493f02d8a665f5b2dedf5ab543475a28b82bc393d3290a60250d26fbb8065b01.svg)\n\nNow, notice that the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n![](images/2c/2c2141d090d129fde56fdfd334e4162927db247703b7ecd62eb14261340ac929.svg)\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n![](images/19/19dfe81d2ed118fd87e4e0ac483119e68f4bbf5ec67ccbaf2614360015ff193a.svg)\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n![](images/0c/0c7d035d915ae4a4cc993d476824897130b85797f32ad753f86e1accd1d5e29e.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177037 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\left( e^{-x^2}- x^2y \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(y+xy^2\\right) \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the unit circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise. \n\nDirect evaluation of this integral is likely to be a difficult task. However, we can make light work of this tricky integral using Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ (shown below) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/dd/dd859c753a8a5445eafab5db4f1e796b48997e1abb1728accd03fe3825e595d6.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}- x^2y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe obtain the partial derivatives   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{-{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis double integral will be easier to evaluate if we switch to plane polar coordinates. \n\nThe region $D$ in $xy$-coordinates can be represented as the region $\\Delta$ in polar coordinates, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral by applying the change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176808}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/1e/1e78bf74df5607a1d80b3bd51b3222505e3bb201817448a7a5ebe5b0a73cffa9.svg)\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176735}}] :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176145}]}
